WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.580
Analysis is where responders turn a possible signal into an informed assessment.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.310
The work includes triage, severity assignment, timeline building, scope review,

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.840
evidence preservation, and stakeholder updates.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.050
Analysts decide what is known, what is likely, what is still unknown,

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.640
and what decision is needed next.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.185
Scope is especially important.

00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:21.270
A single endpoint alert might involve one host, one user, a shared credential,

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.145
a cloud service,

00:00:22.145 --> 00:00:24.160
a business workflow, or a wider pattern.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.190
Analysis should be fast enough to support action but careful enough to avoid unnecessary

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:28.870
disruption.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.200
That means separating facts from assumptions.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.040
For example, an alert may show suspicious login activity,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.245
but the team still needs evidence about source,

00:00:36.245 --> 00:00:40.185
account behavior, session activity, affected resources, and business impact.

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:43.410
Analysts should document confidence and uncertainty in plain language.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.710
They should also preserve evidence before actions change the environment.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.960
Good analysis helps containment become targeted rather than reactive.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.585
It gives leaders a basis for decisions, gives system owners clear next steps,

00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:55.280
and gives the response team a defensible

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.910
record of why actions were taken.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.745
Good analysis is also collaborative.

00:00:58.745 --> 00:01:02.605
A SOC analyst may understand the alert, a system owner may know normal behavior,

00:01:02.605 --> 00:01:05.080
a cloud engineer may understand identity paths,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.400
and a business owner may know operational impact.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.220
Bringing those views together helps the team avoid narrow conclusions and choose actions

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.880
that fit the real environment.
